From: SJ Park <sj@kernel.org>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 23:23:52 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] mm/damon/core: let kdamond freezable

kdamond is not freezable.  If DAMOS is set, it could migrate or reclaim
pages while hibernation image is being copied.  It makes the image
corrupted.  As a result the system could run with corrupted memory after
resuming from the hibernation.

Users could work around by stopping of pausing the kdamond.  But it is
easy to forget or notice.  Just make it freezable.

This change is based on only the theory.  No real issue from such memory
corruption is found and reported so far.

Signed-off-by: SJ Park <sj@kernel.org>
---
 mm/damon/core.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/mm/damon/core.c b/mm/damon/core.c
index 54c527134a54b..ac1ad312d743c 100644
--- a/mm/damon/core.c
+++ b/mm/damon/core.c
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
 
 #include <linux/damon.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/freezer.h>
 #include <linux/kthread.h>
 #include <linux/memcontrol.h>
 #include <linux/mm.h>
@@ -4173,7 +4174,7 @@ static bool kdamond_need_stop(struct damon_ctx *ctx)
 {
 	struct damon_target *t;
 
-	if (kthread_should_stop())
+	if (kthread_freezable_should_stop(NULL))
 		return true;
 
 	if (!ctx->ops.target_valid)
@@ -4497,6 +4498,7 @@ static int kdamond_fn(void *data)
 
 	sz_limit = damon_apply_min_nr_regions(ctx);
 
+	set_freezable();
 	while (!kdamond_need_stop(ctx)) {
 		/*
 		 * ctx->attrs and ctx->next_{aggregation,ops_update}_sis could
-- 
